  <text>
    <body>
      <div>
        <head>In the directories</head>
        <p>The directories list a Victoria Hotel in two places, and they may have been two different hotels. From 1867 to 1872 it is on the corner of <ref target="civic?id=Victoria%20Street">Victoria Street</ref> and <ref target="civic?id=Bouverie%20Street">Bouverie Street</ref>, at No. 21 Victoria Street and No. 1 Bouverie Street, under Peter Kinnane (1867 to 1871). It is listed as vacant in 1872.</p>
        <p>From 1879 to 1920 a Victoria Hotel is listed further along Bouverie Street, at No. 49 (1879 to 1885), No. 73 (1890 to 1915) and No. 75 (1920). The directories list the proprietors as W. Trehey (1879), John Fitzpatrick (1880), William Skardon (1885), John Marzorini (1890), Miss Teresa Dwyer (1895), Thomas Howell (1900), George Strain (1905), Miss Margt. Searson (1910) and Mrs Margt. Nugent (1915 and 1920).</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>Neighbours</head>
        <p>At the Victoria Street corner, Ellen Comans, dressmaker, is at No. 3 Bouverie Street from 1867 to 1872, and F. W. Cullimore, teacher of piano, at No. 19 Victoria Street in 1870 and 1871. In Bouverie Street, E. &amp; A. Hegarty, case dealers, are next to the hotel in 1915, and A. Santospirito at Nos 79–83 in 1920.</p>
      </div>
